{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And they told him, saying.</b> The reason for saying <b>וַיַּגִּידוּ</b> is that the root יגיד connotes difficulty, for they spoke things that were hard for them to say, since in the end their wickedness toward him would be revealed — what they did and what they caused their father, all the grief and weeping. Accordingly, it says וַיַּגִּידוּ — regarding what touched upon them. Or it may be because this news brings about the descent to Egypt and the harsh bondage that embittered the lives of Israel in Egypt, and so you will find that he feared the descent to Egypt, until HaShem said to him (Genesis 46:3): אַל תִּירָא מֵרְדָה מִצְרַיְמָה." "<b>And the reason</b> it says <b>לֵאמֹר</b> is that they schemed to bring him tidings in a manner that would not endanger Jacob, for it is a well-known thing that good news, when it comes upon grief — and all the more so when it is exceedingly abundant with joy — can endanger a person, weakening him so that he suddenly expires. Therefore they were concerned about this and schemed to speak to him in a way that no mishap would occur, and that is the meaning of <b>וַיַּגִּידוּ לוֹ לֵאמֹר</b> — meaning, they told him that they had news to tell him, and through this his soul would expand and his heart rejoice, and only afterward did they tell him <b>עוֹד יוֹסֵף חַי</b>, and there was no danger, for his heart had been prepared to receive good tidings." "Or the verse may be parsed in this manner: <b>וַיַּגִּידוּ לוֹ לֵאמֹר עוֹד</b> — meaning, besides the fact that they came back complete, with Simeon and Benjamin with them, they still had more to tell him. And after they had prepared his mind for something good, they informed him ״יוֹסֵף חַי״, and they further added to say <b>וְכִי הוּא מוֹשֵׁל</b> — one piece of good news after another, for the reason mentioned above." "<b>I saw fit</b> to give attention to the matter of Joseph: how could he have been unconcerned about his father's grief for so many years? It is true that during the days of his servitude we may say that he tried but did not succeed; but after he rose to greatness, why did he not send a letter to his father to relieve his grief? For Joseph knew the extraordinary depth of Jacob's love for him and that he had mourned for him many days. And why did he not send to tell him? And the difficulty grows even greater regarding the years of famine, when people were passing back and forth from the land of Egypt to the land of Canaan, and yet he was unconcerned about his grief. Furthermore, who permitted him, after his brothers had come, to prolong the days of distress and not inform him immediately?" "<b>And it is</b> true that regarding what unfolded through the matters that the Decree-Maker decreed, there are many things that needed to be so: the first — to repay Jacob for the twenty-two years that he did not serve his father, and likewise [Joseph] was in distress twenty-two years; and also for the number רד״ו that HaShem decreed they would be in Egypt; and also for Jacob's descent with great honor, which would not have been so had he descended earlier; and many details that followed. But from Joseph's side, one needs to give a reason for the matter." "<b>And it will be seen</b> that Joseph's reasoning was a reasoning of praise, namely that from the time of his descent to Egypt until the time the word of HaShem, blessed be He, came and brought him out of the house of imprisonment to reign, he had no power to inform his father." "And even if one were to concede that he did have the power, he feared for his life that his brothers would learn of the matter, and out of their shame and humiliation before their father they might surrender themselves against him to destroy him from the world, so that the shame before their father would not reach them." "And it is also possible that he would curse them and put them all to death, and on this account they would seek to uproot him from the world. Therefore he feared for his life that his brothers would learn of the matter, and he refrained from coming at the risk of his life." "And after he emerged from prison and became a great officer and the first fear had passed — even so, he refrained from informing his father. And this is along the lines of what they said, may their memory be for a blessing (Bava Metzi'a 59a): it is better that a person cast himself into a fiery furnace than whiten the face of his fellow. And he was zealous for the honor of the brothers, lest he shame them before Jacob and Isaac and all the seed of Jacob, and he bore it that his father should remain in his grief rather than shame them." "Further, even after he rose to greatness, Joseph reckoned that they still harbored within them a feeling of enmity toward him and would take counsel against him to uproot him from the world. And so you will find that they said, may their memory be for a blessing (Tanchuma), that they sought to uproot him even in the days of his greatness, and Gabriel came and scattered them. And although they said, may their memory be for a blessing (Genesis Rabbah 91), that they went down after him to Egypt to redeem him with dear blood — the matter would be different if they were the ones to redeem him, for then he would recognize their brotherhood; but apart from this, his wrath kept watch forever and he would not forget what they had done to him, and they would preempt him by killing him first. You see, then, that if he had informed Jacob beforehand that he was alive, they would have armed themselves and come and destroyed him from the world." "And even after they came before him in the year of famine, he did not wish to reveal himself immediately to them or to his father, for he was still in danger, as mentioned, until he acted wisely and fed them and gave them drink and provided them with provisions, and showed them that he was with them with a whole heart. Also the matter became revealed, for it had come from HaShem, and they surely knew that he harbored no enmity toward them; and by this means shame was also removed from their father, for they would say to him that the Decree-Maker had so decreed, as I explained in the verse (Genesis 45:8): ״לֹא אַתֶּם״ etc. — and then he revealed himself to them. And even so, HaShem needed to send an angel to deliver him from their hand, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Tanhuma Vayigash). And perhaps it is because he, Joseph, did not know of the matter. Or it may be that he did know that an angel had delivered him, and there is no difficulty to raise against him that he should have informed his father beforehand and not been concerned about the brothers, for the angel who redeemed him now would also have redeemed him before — for one does not rely on a miracle, and in this respect Joseph cannot be blamed for the matter. This is correct."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {4 "The term רד״ו (numerical value 210) refers to the traditional count of years Israel spent in Egypt.", 10 "The phrase דמים in context likely means provisions or money rather than 'bloods'; rendered as 'provisions' following the narrative context of Joseph supplying his brothers."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:49:32.751655406Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}